<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumul Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kumul uprising</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a> Rebellion was an uprising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and other Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> that began at Hami, or Kumul, in 1931 after the provincial governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jin-shuren">Jin Shuren</a> abolished the Kumul Khanate. The rebels called in the Hui warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-zhongying">Ma Zhongying</a>, and the revolt spread across the province, helping to spark the short-lived First East Turkestan Republic in 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumul-rebellion.jpg" alt="Kumul Rebellion" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, photographed in the Republic of China · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkic_Conscripts_36th_division_1933_Kumul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jin-shuren">Jin Shuren</a> became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> after the assassination of Yang Zengxin in 1928. He openly antagonised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and Kyrgyz with travel restrictions, higher taxes, seizures of property and frequent executions. Pressed for money and crowded with Han refugees from warlord fighting elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, he annexed the semi-autonomous Kumul Khanate, whose khans were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagataids</a>, last ruling descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. Kumulik land was taken and given to Han settlers, and rebellion broke out on 20 February 1931, with many Han killed by the local population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Yulbars Khan, an adviser at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a> court, appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-zhongying">Ma Zhongying</a>, a Hui warlord in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, who had a secret understanding with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> that he would be recognised as ruler of the province if he won it. Ma besieged Kumul but could not take the city and, wounded in October, withdrew to Gansu. Jin bought two Soviet biplanes and signed a secret treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in October 1931, and provincial troops relieved Kumul on 30 November 1931.</p><p>In the south a separate rising in Khotan, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-amin-bughra">Muhammad Amin Bughra</a> and his brothers, sought complete independence and was hostile to both Han and Hui. On 20 February 1933 a provisional government was set up there with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabit-damulla">Sabit Damulla Abdulbaki</a> as prime minister. Its forces expelled Swedish missionaries, persecuted Christian converts and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> in Karghalik and Khotan. In April 1933 White Russian troops in Ürümqi overthrew Jin and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheng-shicai">Sheng Shicai</a>. Ma Zhongying returned, his troops notorious for looting and burning villages, and with Zhang Peiyuan attacked Sheng in the Second Battle of Ürümqi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Sheng called on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, whose troops crossed the border in January 1934 and drove Ma back from Ürümqi with aerial bombing; Zhang Peiyuan was defeated and took his own life. Ma's retreating Hui forces turned south and destroyed the East Turkestan government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a> and Yangi Hissar. In February 1934 between 1,700 and 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> civilians in Kashgar Old City were massacred in revenge for the Kizil massacre of Han and Hui. Ma Zhongying's brother-in-law Ma Hushan then governed southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in the name of the central government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kumul Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
